const description = 'I compare the Round Lab Birch Juice sunscreen, sun serum and Mild-Up, plus the stick as a bonus. My honest review: texture, white cast, under makeup, mineral vs chemical, waterproof, Korean vs US versions, and which to buy.';
const faq = [
  ['Which Round Lab Birch Juice sunscreen is the best?', 'For me the sun serum is the best: it is light, absorbs fast, leaves no white cast and sits best under makeup. The regular sunscreen is a close second and a safe pick for almost everyone. Mild-Up is for people who like a tone-up effect, and the stick is for reapplying on the go.'],
  ['Is Round Lab Birch Juice sunscreen mineral or chemical?', 'It depends on the version. On Round Lab’s US store, the regular Birch sunscreen, the sun serum and the sun stick list chemical filters, while Mild-Up lists zinc oxide, a mineral filter. Filters can differ between the Korean and US versions, so always check the box you own.'],
  ['Does Round Lab Birch Juice sunscreen leave a white cast?', 'Not in my experience with the regular sunscreen, the sun serum or the stick. Mild-Up is thicker than the regular sunscreen and leaves a slight white cast and a brightening tone-up effect, which I personally do not love.'],
  ['Is Round Lab Birch Juice sunscreen waterproof?', 'On my skin, water droplets bead up on the surface and do not soak in, so it behaves as if it is waterproof. Officially, though, no sunscreen is truly waterproof. Water-resistant labels are only 40 or 80 minutes, so I reapply after swimming or sweating.'],
  ['Does it work under makeup?', 'Yes. The sun serum is the most perfect one under makeup for me, so start there if you wear makeup every day. The brand also says the stick is made for reapplying over makeup.'],
  ['Do I need to reapply Round Lab sunscreen?', 'In normal city life I do not reapply the sunscreen, sun serum or Mild-Up. The stick I do reapply. In strong sun I reapply about every 2 to 3 hours depending on the UV index, and the American Academy of Dermatology recommends every 2 hours.'],
  ['How much should I put on my face?', 'I use the two-finger rule. The American Academy of Dermatology says at least about a teaspoon for the face, roughly the length of your index and middle fingers. Don’t forget your neck, ears and the backs of your hands.'],
  ['What is the difference between the Korean and US versions?', 'The filter lists I found for the US store and for the original Korean formula are not identical. I use the Korean versions. On the US store, filters like avobenzone, homosalate and octisalate are listed, while Round Lab’s own article on the original sunscreen names Uvinul A Plus, Uvinul T 150, Uvasorb HEB and Tinosorb M. Compare the ingredient list on your own box.'],
  ['Is it good for sensitive skin and oily skin?', 'My skin is normal, and I had no stinging, breakouts or clogged pores with any of the four. I can’t speak for oily or very sensitive skin, so patch-test first, and ask your dermatologist if you have acne or a skin condition.'],
  ['Would I buy it again?', 'Yes, absolutely, as soon as I finish the ones I have.'],
];
